{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1785185830,"nonce":"hydx2h3oPheVGy/eFijhnKAltTs=","title":"ADR-0062 BLOCKER: fsGroup does not apply to NFS volumes, so a Step may not be able to write its own Workspace","message":"Found by red-teaming ADR-0062, before any Export code existed. **This may be a blocker for part 2** and it should be answered before `3a0f2a2` (s5) or `0ea908b` (s6) are built.\n\nThe executor sets Pod-level `fs_group: WORKSPACE_GID` (`crates/scarab-executor-k8s/src/lib.rs:2082,2869`) and the comment states the restored `/workspace` is \"owned by `WORKSPACE_GID` and only group-writable\". The step's uid is image-defined (`:3049`), so group ownership is what grants write access today.\n\n**The in-tree NFS plugin reports `Managed: false`, so kubelet applies no fsGroup ownership to an NFS volume.** Write access then depends entirely on AUTH_SYS uid/gid matching the file modes — and ADR-0061 s7 preserves modes *exactly*, so an inherited `0644` file is not group-writable by anyone but its owner.\n\nADR-0062 discusses AUTH_SYS only as a *fence* problem and never as an access-control problem for the Step itself. Verify against a real export and then decide: export-side uid/gid squashing (`all_squash`/`anonuid`), setting ownership when the Farm is built, `fsGroupPolicy`-equivalent behaviour, or something else. Whatever is chosen must not break s7's pinned mode fidelity (`crates/scarab-storage-s3/tests/fidelity.rs`).\n\n\n- [ ] Demonstrated, with a real export and a real unprivileged Step-shaped Pod, whether the Step can write\n- [ ] If it cannot, a fix that preserves ADR-0061 s7 mode fidelity — mode fidelity is a pinned contract, not a preference\n- [ ] Works for an image-defined uid the author chose, not only for Scarab's default\n- [ ] The answer recorded in ADR-0062, which currently does not mention this at all\n\n\nNone - answer this before building s5/s6","files":null}]}